{"type": "init", "tier": "prototype", "idea": "A phone-first English self-study app for one Vietnamese grade-8 learner where a story read chapter by chapter is the spine and the syllabus is carried by it, built on a clean-room curriculum design derived from the research base rather than ported from the current system", "at": "2026-08-16T15:18:23.859Z"}
{"type": "note", "text": "Rules housekeeping done: .atelier/rules/design-foundations.md and patterns.md rewritten from shipped starter templates to product-specific bars (learner-facing surface explains nothing; no band number; story is spine not wrapper; support withdraws once per item; committed marked attempt not reveal-and-self-mark; phone-first at 360px; one gesture moves one thing; real-browser geometry gates; coverage emitted beside every sweep; no porting app.js). They feed the extract/elevate prompts at /promote.", "at": "2026-08-16T15:18:38.072Z"}
{"type": "note", "text": "Prior art scan for the accountability milestone (the d=0.51 vs d=0.01 fork, Sangers 2025 [V 3-0]). Four candidate forms, each with a named failure mode: (1) STREAK/daily cadence - strong retention prior art but brittle, one missed day collapses the mechanic for many users; (2) IN-STORY GATE - the next chapter is earned by answering what happened, accountability hidden inside the fiction, no separate homework surface; (3) OWNED LOG/artefact - a visible record the learner keeps (the story's own twelve chalk marks are a native fit), Matthews' written-goal-plus-weekly-report 76% vs 43%; (4) HUMAN ON THE OTHER END - the operator sees the log. Repeated finding across sources: app-plus-one-human-partner is the effective configuration, and this product uniquely HAS that human. Counter-evidence to carry: end-of-reading quizzes are documented as demotivating/punitive when they test detail (Edutopia), so the accountability step must be about what happened, not detail recall.", "at": "2026-08-16T15:18:38.227Z"}
